# modules.cpp shell specification

# Status and authority

This document specifies the target-safe mm.shell foundation, the mm.shell.mcu
level-2 composition, their APIs, and caller-owned storage rules.

# Architectural boundaries

mm.shell is the target-safe, allocation-free level-1 foundation. It has no
dependencies on filesystem, environment, process management, dynamic heap
allocation, or platform-specific drivers. It builds for both host and embedded
targets.

Legacy POSIX shell services reside in mm.shell.posix and are consumed by
tools/shell. There is no dependency path from mm.shell to mm.shell.posix.

mm.shell.mcu adds only mm.mcu and mm.stdio to the embedded closure. Its
commands allocate nothing and do not import POSIX services.

# Storage ownership rules

All persistent and scratch memory is owned by the application or caller and
passed into mm.shell as explicit spans:

- mm.shell performs no dynamic allocations (no malloc, new, or STL containers).
- Storage capacities are caller-bounded and declared in application arrays.
- Handlers preflight transaction scratch capacity before performing side effects
  or writing output. An insufficient scratch buffer returns Status::Overflow
  with no partial output and no hardware side effect.
- Registry descriptors are stored in caller-provided spans. install_pack
  preflights all entries, performing atomic all-or-none registration.

## Level-1 acceptance storage profile

apps/shell-smoke is the named level-1 acceptance application. It declares every
storage class the level-1 API owns as a static array, installs the complete
standard pack plus one custom handler and one installed script, and runs a fixed
boundary script that exercises assignment, a conditional, a loop, checked
arithmetic, a function with a positional argument, command substitution, a
custom native handler, an installed script, and yield.

Its source and parse capacities, with the requirement measured from that script
beside each one:

- source bytes: 512, measured 220
- parser tokens: 64, measured 54
- word fragments: 48, measured 34
- syntax nodes: 72, measured 60
- syntax links: 72, measured 59
- parser context frames: 16, measured 9

The application measures the script before committing any parse storage and
exits rather than discovering a short array mid-parse, so these measured values
are checked on every run rather than only recorded here.

The remaining capacities are declared with headroom rather than tuned to this
one script's minima, because they are the application's operating budget and not
a property of the fixture:

- variables: 16 slots, 256 bytes
- positional parameters: 16 slots, 128 bytes
- expansion pieces: 64; generated bytes: 128
- expanded fields: 32 slots, 128 bytes
- assignment shadow variables: 16 slots, 256 bytes
- argument vector: 32 slots, 256 bytes
- evaluator frames: 16
- loop items: 16 slots, 128 bytes
- case pattern bytes: 64
- prefix-assignment variables: 16 slots, 256 bytes
- call positional slots: 32
- command-substitution capture bytes: 256; capture values: 8
- capture child variables: 16 slots, 256 bytes
- capture parse arena: 32 tokens, 32 fragments, 32 nodes, 32 links, 16 frames
- functions: 4 slots, 256 arena bytes, 32/32/32/32 parse arena, 16 frames
- capture functions: the same capacities again
- installed scripts: 2 slots, 32/32/32/32 parse arena, 16 frames
- command descriptors: 24
- staged ordinary-output bytes: 256
- staged diagnostic-output bytes: 256
- application output bytes: 512
- transaction scratch bytes: 256

A capacity failure exits with 64 plus the StorageClass ordinal, so a run names
the array to grow rather than only reporting that something was short.

apps/mcu-shell adds console and Session storage at level 2. Flash and
static-RAM budgets are measured in platform lanes, not on the host.

## Level-2 MCU composition

mm.shell.mcu installs ten descriptors with the level-1 pack as one atomic
registry transaction: board, gpio, spi, i2c, uart, ticks, delay, adc, pwm,
and console. A short registry installs neither pack. Descriptors remain
installed when a capability is absent, so help can still describe them.
Dispatch checks the exact required capability before reaching a provider.
There is no time descriptor; ticks is the monotonic clock query.

The capability set starts with mandatory level-1 software features and merges
Board, Gpio, Spi, I2c, Uart, Timer, Adc, and Pwm from the selected mm.mcu
provider. Console is present when McuConsole successfully initializes and
attaches to an mm.stdio driver. Physical carrier connection is separate:
console connected prints 0 or 1 after attachment, even when the terminal is
disconnected. With no attached driver, console dispatch returns status 125.

McuConsole keeps a caller-owned pending-byte span. Its ByteSink accepts a
complete write or returns WouldBlock, never a partial logical write. The
application pumps the queue through mm.stdio and retains a short physical
write for the next iteration. apps/mcu-shell owns 512 pending bytes, 64 input
bytes, 512 ordinary-output bytes, 256 diagnostic-output bytes, and 512
transaction-scratch bytes.
It feeds Session and steps the evaluator once per event-loop iteration.
The larger scratch span fits the 48 named GPIO/PWM entries on RP2350B; a
256-byte span cannot hold that inventory as one atomic output line set.

The installed blink and sample scripts require Gpio plus Timer, and Adc,
respectively. Their measured parser high-water counts are:

Opening fence
script   tokens  fragments  nodes  links  context
blink        23         18     29     28        4
sample       16         12     22     21        8
combined     39         30     51     49       12
Closing fence

The application reserves 96 entries in each script token, fragment, node,
and link arena, plus 16 context frames. Host tests measure both scripts and
install them in an exact-size combined arena to guard against silent growth.
The same host suite runs both scripts through Session against recording MCU
providers, including ADC command substitution, with no physical board.

All MCU numbers are unsigned decimal except I2C addresses and transfer bytes,
which also accept 0x hexadecimal. Read queries print one line: 0 or 1 for
GPIO and console state, a raw count for ADC, nanoseconds for PWM period, a
tick count for ticks, and lowercase two-digit hex bytes separated by spaces
for SPI and I2C reads. Board prints its name; GPIO, ADC, and PWM inventories
print NUMBER NAME lines. Mutations print nothing on success. The query output
is assembled in caller scratch before it is offered to the output sink.

Bad syntax and provider BadArgument return status 2. A provider returning
Unsupported after claiming its capability is a contract failure (70).
TransportError is 74, Busy is 75, Timeout is 124, and an absent capability
is 125. Transaction scratch overflow records Status::Overflow and the exact
required byte count, before any provider call. Diagnostics use the error sink
and do not contaminate captured stdout.

### Pico SDK release-size qualification

The shell-free apps/mcu-shell-size-control links the same platform and stdio
providers. Flash is the release load image reported by the default
arm-none-eabi-size or riscv32-pico-elf-size summary. Static RAM is the sum of
ELF .data and .bss section sizes from size -A, excluding the reserved heap.
The Pico bridge labels .data executable, so the default size summary groups
it under text and reports data as zero. Using that zero as the RAM figure
would miss the application's static storage. Sizes below are bytes:

Opening fence
lane          shell flash  control flash  delta flash  shell RAM
RP2040             162548          49808       112740      58704
RP2350 Arm         157328          47592       109736      58592
RP2350 RISC-V      206680          42688       163992      73780
Closing fence

The normative RP2040 ceilings are 256 KiB total flash, 160 KiB incremental
flash, and 64 KiB static RAM. Its measured values meet all three. Each lane
linked both ELFs and produced the Pico SDK UF2 flashable artifact. These are
compile/link qualifications, not hardware execution results. The interactive
Pico console script was not run, at the user's request. The M68k QEMU
and MPS2 Arm QEMU shell-smoke regressions both passed. The MPS2 board
explicitly reports entropy unavailable through its newlib syscall boundary;
it never invents random bytes for the sake of linking.

# Foundation API

# Full-profile syntax boundary

mm.shell.full is a separate, target-buildable module with no POSIX header or
system call. FullScript owns source bytes, source-relative tokens, an owning
syntax tree, and here-document records. Moving a FullScript cannot dangle its
source ranges. FullState owns the buffers backing a ShellState view plus host
directory, export-name, and trap metadata; a child fork copies visible
positionals and variables into its own buffers.

The full grammar adds pipelines, input/output/append and descriptor
redirections, parenthesized subshells, compound commands, and here-documents.
It retains embedded constructs and parses all 36 tracked /bin/sh scripts.
The tracked corpus has one here-document, cat <<PLAN in scripts/release.sh;
a fixture separately covers a quoted delimiter. <<- is unsupported. An
unquoted delimiter permits body expansion at evaluation time; a quoted one
suppresses it. The parser retains that flag and the exact body span.

Full-profile helpers recognize hexadecimal arithmetic literals within the
same signed 64-bit limit and implement the four positional pattern trims
through the core pattern matcher. A corpus parse proves syntax admission, not
that the script has executed.

## Full evaluation against abstract services

Everything below runs against function-pointer services, so mm.shell.full is
exercised entirely with fakes and still holds no POSIX header or symbol. The
service surfaces are IoService for descriptors and bytes, ProcessService for
spawn, wait, and signal, SignalService for install, restore, and poll, and
FileService for directory names. A test supplies each one directly.

### Pipelines

run_pipeline executes one pipeline from a span of ProcessRequests. The last
stage supplies the pipeline status and every earlier stage is still reaped. A
failed launch closes every parent pipe end, signals the children it did start,
and waits for each of them, so a service failure leaks no descriptor and no
child handle. Fakes control completion order, partial writes, signals, and
statuses without forking.

### Redirection planning

plan_redirections turns one command's redirection tokens into an ordered plan
and performs no I/O. Operands stay in their raw shell spelling, because
expansion happens before any service call and the evaluator must verify every
request before it alters the active descriptors. A here-document step carries
its body and whether the delimiter permitted expansion.

### Here-document bodies

expand_here_document turns a body into a byte stream, never a field vector,
which is how the no-splitting rule is enforced structurally rather than by
convention. A quoted delimiter yields the body verbatim as one literal segment.
An unquoted one expands parameters, including braced forms and their `:-` and
`:+` operands, and arithmetic including hexadecimal, and honours the backslash
escapes a here-document recognizes: `\$`, `` \` ``, `\\`, and a line
continuation. Every other backslash is a literal byte. Neither variant performs
field splitting or pathname expansion.

A command substitution inside a body is returned as a Substitution segment
holding the inner source, because running a list is the evaluator's work and
planning does none. The tracked corpus needs exactly that: scripts/release.sh
interpolates `$(git log -1 ...)` inside its `cat <<PLAN` body. An assignment
operand such as `${name:=other}` is Unsupported, because it would mutate state a
body only reads, and the obsolete backtick form is refused here as it is in the
parser.

### Pathname expansion

expand_pathname asks only for names. A word with no pattern byte costs no
service call and reports no expansion. A word with one is split on `/` and each
component matched through the core pattern matcher, so a literal component
still needs no listing. Matches are byte-sorted, which is what a shell in the
POSIX locale produces, and a leading period is matched only by a literal one.

An unmatched pattern reports no expansion rather than an error, which is the
POSIX rule that leaves the word unchanged. A directory the service reports as
missing or forbidden contributes no match; any other service failure is
reported, so an empty result is never mistaken for a broken service.

### Traps

configure_trap takes the argument vector including `trap` and its action. Every
condition is validated before a provider or the owning state changes, and a
failed provider call rolls back the conditions it had already changed. The
implemented grammar is exactly the four pinned forms:

Opening fence
trap restore_host 0
trap - 0
trap mm_cleanup EXIT INT TERM
trap - EXIT INT TERM
Closing fence

Numeric 0 and symbolic EXIT name the same condition, 2 and INT the same, 15 and
TERM the same. A mixed condition list is valid and a repeated condition is
refused. `-` restores the default action for every named condition. EXIT is a
shell event, so it installs no operating-system hook; only INT and TERM reach
SignalService. Handler state belongs to the shell child that installed it, so a
child's reset leaves its parent's action in place. poll_trap resolves a pending
signal against the owning state's action.

### Full-profile functions

FullFunctionLibrary keeps mm.shell's lookup, atomic replacement, and lifetime
contract and differs only in owning its storage. A body is parsed into its own
FullScript before the visible handle changes, so a failed or unsupported
redefinition leaves the previous body visible. The handle is shared, so a body
that is executing when it is redefined keeps running the text it started with.

## Host POSIX services

mm.shell.posix supplies the concrete providers for the abstract services
mm.shell.full declares. HostServices owns every host resource it hands out:
open descriptors, live children, and installed signal dispositions. Its
destructor closes and restores whatever it still owns, so an abandoned command
leaks nothing.

No POSIX type crosses an exported declaration. A handle is an index into
HostServices' own tables rather than a descriptor or a process id, and every
POSIX header lives in a private source unit: io.cpp, process.cpp, signal.cpp,
and file.cpp. open_descriptors, live_children, and installed_signals report what
is still owned, which is how a leak assertion is written after a deliberate
failure. borrow_descriptor adopts a descriptor the shell did not open, such as
the process's own standard streams, and closing that handle releases only the
slot.

### Child setup and failure classes

Child setup reports a pre-exec failure through a dedicated close-on-exec pipe.
A successful exec closes that pipe with nothing written, which is how the parent
sees success; otherwise the child writes the stage that failed and its errno and
exits. The parent reaps that child itself and publishes no handle, so three
outcomes stay distinct rather than collapsing into one error:

- NotFound for a program that does not exist, which the shell maps to 127;
- PermissionDenied for one that cannot be executed, which maps to 126;
- Failed for an infrastructure fault such as a descriptor or directory setup
  error, which is neither.

A waited child reports its exit status, or 128 plus the signal number when a
signal ended it. Waiting on an already-reaped handle is Invalid.

### Descriptors, names, and signals

A write reports the count it moved rather than looping, because the shell
boundary distinguishes Accepted from WouldBlock and the pump owns retrying. End
of file is a zero count, not a failure. An unknown handle is Invalid rather than
a host error.

The file service reports names only: pathname expansion opens nothing and reads
no byte. A missing name is an answer rather than a service failure, which is
what keeps an unmatched pattern from looking like a broken provider.

Signal dispositions are process-wide, so at most one HostServices may install
them at a time and the instance that installed one restores it. The handler
touches only a volatile sig_atomic_t pending set. Dispositions install without
SA_RESTART, so a pending condition interrupts a blocking read and the shell can
act on it between commands. poll reports the lowest pending condition and
consumes it, which keeps a run deterministic.

## Full-profile word expansion

expand_word_full turns one word's raw shell spelling into fields: quote
handling, parameter expansion with every full-profile operator, arithmetic,
command substitution, field splitting, pathname expansion, and quote removal.
The caller says whether splitting and pathname expansion apply, because POSIX
suppresses both for an assignment value, a here-document operand, a case
selector, and a redirection target.

Running a nested list is not expansion's job, so `$(list)` is delegated to a
Substituter the interpreter supplies. Its status is reported back, which is what
a command consisting only of a substitution exits with. Without a Substituter a
substitution is Unsupported, and the obsolete backtick form is refused
everywhere.

### The quoting mask

Expansion accumulates bytes with a per-byte quoting mask rather than a list of
pieces. That mask is what lets the two later stages tell a literal
metacharacter from an expanded one:

- field splitting breaks on an unquoted IFS byte only, and additionally at a
  recorded boundary so that `"$@"` stays one field per argument whatever IFS
  says;
- pathname expansion runs on a field holding an unquoted pattern byte.

A quoted section counts even when it is empty, which is the difference between
`""` and `"$unset"` yielding one empty field each and an unquoted empty
expansion yielding none at all.

A field mixing quoted and unquoted metacharacters, such as `"*"x*`, is left
literal rather than risking a quoted metacharacter acting as a wildcard. That is
a deliberate deviation: the safe direction is to under-expand.

### Parameter operators

The three colon forms come from mm.shell. The full profile adds `${#name}`, the
plain `-`, `+`, `=` forms that treat only an unset parameter as unset, `:?`, and
the four pattern trims `#`, `##`, `%`, `%%` through trim_parameter. An operand
is itself a word, so it expands by the same rules with splitting and pathname
expansion off, bounded by a recursion limit. `$#`, `$?`, `$$`, a positional, and
both argument-vector forms are resolved directly; `$!` and `$-` are absent
because there is no job control and no option string at this level.

## The full-profile interpreter

Interpreter walks a FullScript and executes it against the abstract services.
Recursion here is ordinary C++ recursion: the cooperative step budget belongs to
the embedded profile, where an application pumps between commands, not to a host
shell with a real stack.

It drives every piece above: expand_word_full for words, plan_redirections for
descriptors, expand_here_document for bodies, run_pipeline for stages,
configure_trap and poll_trap for conditions, FullFunctionLibrary for
definitions, and FullState::fork_into for a child.

### Simple commands

Words live in a Simple node's token range, because the parser records only
redirections as children; a token inside a redirection's range is not a command
word. Leading assignment words are expanded without splitting or pathname
expansion. With no command word the assignments persist and the status is that
of the last substitution their values ran.

Resolution is function first, then builtin, then the host. A prefix assignment
persists for a special builtin and for a function, and is restored afterwards
otherwise, which is what makes `IFS= read -r line` leave IFS as it was.

Redirections are applied by building the command's stream set rather than by
duplicating descriptors in the shell and restoring them. A builtin writes to
that set and an external receives it in its ProcessRequest, so nothing has to be
undone and a failure cannot leave the shell's own streams altered. A
here-document becomes a pipe the shell fills before the command runs.

### Pipelines

The first stage runs in this shell with its output on a pipe, so a builtin such
as echo or printf can feed the rest, and every later stage is one external
process wired by the service. A builtin or function after a pipe is Unsupported
rather than approximated, because it would need a process no abstract service
offers. A survey of the tracked corpus confirms that is sufficient: every
pipeline there is a builtin or external followed by externals.

### set -e and tested contexts

errexit is suppressed by a depth counter rather than a flag threaded through
every production. The counter rises for a condition, an inverted command, and
every non-final `&&` or `||` operand, so the whole subtree of a condition is
exempt, which is what dash does. `if false; then ...; fi`, `false || recovered`,
and `! false` therefore do not end a script under set -e, while a plain failing
command does.

### Builtins

`:`, true, false, echo, printf, test, `[`, pwd, cd, exit, return, break,
continue, shift, set, export, trap, read, command, and exec. These are the ones
the tracked corpus uses; `local`, `eval`, `until`, `getopts`, and `type` are
absent because no tracked script uses them.

Two behaviours are deliberate deviations, both documented rather than hidden:

- exec runs its program and exits with its status instead of replacing the
  process image, because no abstract service replaces a process. For a script
  whose last action is exec, which is the corpus's only use, the observable
  result is the same;
- restoring a prefix-assigned name that had no previous value assigns empty
  rather than unsetting it, which is the closest this level gets to unset and is
  what `IFS=` needs.

### Command substitution and subshells

A substitution pipes the child's ordinary output back, forks the value state so
the child cannot mutate the parent, and adopts the parent's function handles.
The whole capture is read before the status is reported, so a child that wrote
more than one pipe buffer is never truncated. Trailing newlines are removed.
`$?` is not updated by a substitution partway through a word, which matches
dash: a later word in the same command still sees the previous status.

A subshell forks the same way, so its assignments, working directory, and traps
stay inside it while its external side effects are real. exit inside a subshell
ends only the subshell.

## The native shell CLI

tools/shell executes scripts natively. Its modes are:

Opening fence
shell [--project MANIFEST] --check FILE...
shell [--project MANIFEST] --tokens FILE
shell [--project MANIFEST] --dump-ast FILE
shell [--project MANIFEST] --run FILE [-- ARG...]
shell [--project MANIFEST] -c TEXT [-- ARG...]
shell [--project MANIFEST] --profile=embedded --run FILE [-- ARG...]
shell [--project MANIFEST] --capabilities
shell [--project MANIFEST] --commands
Closing fence

MANIFEST defaults to mm.mdy and is resolved before any script runs, so native
policy always has a root. -c carries no implicit project provenance. Every
argument after `--` is preserved as one positional value. -e still sets an
initial exported variable. Exactly one mode may be given.

--check, --tokens, and --dump-ast are parser-only; --capabilities and --commands
are introspection over the level's capability set and the installed pack.
--run and -c evaluate through the interpreter over the host services:
`mm::build::run`, `std::system`, and /bin/sh are gone from those paths. A script
exits with its own status, so `-c 'exit 7'` exits 7.

The `[MANIFEST] COMMAND` positional form remains for one migration release as an
alias for -c. It prints a deprecation diagnostic and uses the native parser,
not /bin/sh.

### The temporary rollback

--legacy-sh is an explicit, host-only rollback for one release. It delegates to
/bin/sh through the process service, never through system(), prints a
deprecation diagnostic to stderr, and cannot be combined with
--profile=embedded.

It is never selected automatically. A native parse failure, such as a backtick
substitution, reports its diagnostic and exits with a usage code; a native
runtime failure, such as a missing program, reports 127 to the script. Neither
reaches /bin/sh. Verified with an execve trace: --legacy-sh executes /bin/sh
exactly once, and -c executes it zero times.

Removal requires a later plan after one released migration cycle.

The dash differential harness is the remainder of this change set.

# Foundation API

## Status and flow

- Status: classifies internal operation outcomes (Ok, BadArgument, Duplicate,
  NotFound, Unavailable, Unsupported, Overflow, WriteError, ReadError).
- CommandStatus: standard exit codes (Ok = 0, Failure = 1, Usage = 2,
  Unavailable = 125, CannotExecute = 126, NotFound = 127).
- Flow: execution control flow (Normal, Exit, Break, Continue, Return, Replace,
  Yield).
- CommandResult: combines Flow, an integer exit status, a typed Status error,
  and OverflowInfo identifying the exhausted storage class and required count.

## Capabilities

Capability flags govern command availability and runtime permissions.
CapabilitySet represents active or required capabilities as a bitset with no
heap allocation:

- Level selects the BareMetal, Mcu, or Posix software composition.
- ScriptProfile independently selects Embedded or Full language semantics.

- level1: core scripts, custom commands, variables, functions, substitution.
- level2: mandatory Level 1 software capabilities. Peripheral drivers (board,
  gpio, spi, i2c, uart, timer, adc, pwm, console) are optional and
  provider-derived, merged dynamically from mm.mcu.
- level3: Level 2 software capabilities plus host services (pathname, files,
  processes, pipelines, redirections, signals, environment). Level 3 does
  not falsely claim MCU hardware capabilities.

## I/O services

IoServices provides out and err ByteSink channels. ByteSink encapsulates a void*
context pointer and write and flush function pointers returning SinkResult.
MemorySink provides an in-memory buffer adapter that safely rejects writes
exceeding capacity without partial stream corruption. Its failure query returns
Status::Overflow, the configured StorageClass, and the required byte count.
DiscardSink provides an explicit sink that accepts all writes without retaining
them.

## Registry and dispatch

Registry maintains a linear table of CommandDescriptor records in caller-owned
storage. install enforces uniqueness, non-SpecialBuiltin classification, and
valid names. install_pack ensures atomic preflight validation before mutation.
Only an internal mm.shell partition can atomically install the standard pack
that owns SpecialBuiltin descriptors. Application code has no such entry point.
The exact name `[` is admitted for the required embedded test builtin; brackets
inside other descriptor names remain invalid. dispatch verifies nonempty argv
matching the descriptor name and required capabilities before invoking the
handler.

## Bounded word expansion in progress

expand_word consumes scanner fragments and caller-owned piece, generated-text,
field, and optional shadow-variable spans. It resolves scalar parameters,
positionals, checked arithmetic, and operands of `:-`, `:+`, and `:=`.
Nested scalar parameters are evaluated recursively with a fixed depth
bound. Assignment uses a variable fork capped at the parent's capacities;
state is committed only after field output succeeds. Failure leaves both
state and published fields unchanged. Argument-vector forms inside operands
and command substitution currently return Status::Unsupported. They are not
yet Level 1 conformance claims.

A command-substitution fragment consumes one entry of
WordExpansionStorage::substitutions, the text the evaluator captured before the
word was expanded, in fragment order. The result reports how many entries the
word used, so the caller can advance to the next word. An empty span makes such
a fragment Status::Unsupported, which is what a caller that cannot run a nested
list wants.

expand_value is the same expansion without field splitting, for assignment
values and case selectors. Its value_offset argument drops an unquoted
literal prefix such as NAME=, so no separate fragment copy is needed. Only a
Literal fragment may straddle that boundary; anything else is BadArgument.
The result publishes one field or none, and more than one field is
Status::Unsupported at this level.

## Function storage in progress

FunctionLibrary owns caller-supplied slots, copied source bytes, and monotonic
token, fragment, node, and link arenas. define measures and validates a body,
preflights every arena, copies the name and body source, parses into owned
storage, then publishes or replaces one visible handle. A failed definition
leaves the old handle visible. Old allocations are reclaimed on reset, not on
replacement.

adopt copies another library's visible handles without copying its arena, which
is how a command-substitution child starts from the parent's definitions and
still defines its own in separate storage. The source arena must outlive every
adopted handle.

A definition executed by the evaluator hands define the body's list source with
the braces stripped, not the brace group itself. The copy is the same script
either way, and a call then costs one frame instead of a redundant group
layer.

## Installed scripts in progress

ScriptDescriptor is the script equivalent of CommandDescriptor: a name, a
summary, a required CapabilitySet, and a SourceView. Unlike a function
definition, installed source is borrowed rather than copied, so the name,
summary, and source bytes must outlive the library.

ScriptLibrary owns caller-supplied slots and a monotonic parse arena. install
validates the name, rejects a duplicate, rejects a name a native command
already owns when the registry is passed to it, measures the body, preflights
every arena, parses, and commits one descriptor and script together. Any
failure restores every cursor, so nothing partially installed is ever visible.
install_pack validates and measures against the cursors each previous entry
would leave, and rolls the whole pack back on any failure. There is no
replacement or removal; reset reclaims the arena.

An absent runtime capability does not prevent installation. The invocation
reports Unavailable, status 125, before the script's first command runs. An
empty body installs as a no-op script, exactly as an empty script file is for a
real shell.

## Evaluator in progress

Evaluator begins from an EmbeddedScript and caller-owned Registry,
CommandContext, and EvaluatorStorage. EvaluatorStorage supplies expansion
scratch, argv slots and argv text, structural frames, a loop-item arena with
its text pool, case pattern bytes, the variable fork used to revert
non-persistent prefix assignments, and the staged ordinary and diagnostic
output spans. Every span is caller-owned and non-aliasing, and the evaluator
allocates nothing.

### Frames and stepping

A frame identifies a syntax index and a resumption phase, never a C++ call
frame. The frame kinds are List, AndOr, If, While, For, Case, Brace, and
Function. Each frame also names the EmbeddedScript its indices belong to,
because a function body is a separate script owned by the FunctionLibrary. Each
frame records the loop-arena marks it was pushed with, so popping it reclaims
exactly the items it added. Exhausting the frame span fails with
Status::Overflow and StorageClass::EvaluatorFrames.

step performs structural transitions or invokes one native handler, never
both. It consumes at most one work unit per structural transition and one
further unit per sixteen bytes of resumable evaluator copying: word-list
materialization for `for`, selector capture for `case`, and pattern byte
assembly. Long scans retain their cursor in the owning frame and resume on
the next call. Exhausting the budget returns Running rather than failure, and
a budget of zero makes no progress.

Argument-vector expansion for one simple command is not charged in units. Like
the work inside a native handler, it is bounded by declared capacity instead:
the caller's argv slot and argv text spans. Exceeding either is Overflow with
StorageClass::ExpandedFields or ExpandedFieldText before any dispatch, so a
maximum-capacity command cannot outrun its storage.

Four accessors make the contract testable, each describing the call that just
returned. last_step_units and last_step_handlers cover structural work: a step
never reports more units than the budget it was given and never reports two
handler entries. last_command_slots and last_command_bytes report what the last
simple command consumed of its argv capacities. Adversarial maximum-capacity
fixtures assert all four.

### Implemented productions

- lists, `&&`, `||`, and negation, including negation of a compound command;
- assignment-only commands, which expand without field splitting;
- prefix assignments, visible to the handler for that invocation only and
  reverted afterwards unless the descriptor is a SpecialBuiltin;
- `if`, `elif`, and `else`, which yield status 0 when no branch runs;
- `while`, whose status is that of the last body command, or 0 if none ran;
- `for`, over an explicit word list or, with no `in` clause, over the
  positional parameters;
- `case`, with alternative patterns and quoted metacharacters. A pattern byte
  produced by expansion is compared literally at this level;
- brace groups.

### Functions and call frames

Function definition and lookup are available only when EvaluatorStorage carries
a FunctionLibrary. Without one a definition reports Status::Unsupported and the
name never resolves.

Executing a definition rejects the name of a special builtin, then measures and
deep-copies the body into the library. Replacing a definition leaves the old
one visible until the new copy is complete.

Name resolution at a simple command is: a special builtin first, then a
function, then an installed script, then any other installed command, then
status 127. A call is a frame, not a handler, so it consumes no handler budget.
Prefix assignments to a function or installed script persist after it returns,
exactly as they do for a special builtin.

`run NAME ARG...` selects only an installed script, bypassing functions and
native names, and gives the script NAME as its `$0`. The evaluator resolves that
descriptor itself, because pushing a call frame is not something a handler can
do; invokes_script identifies it, so the behavior is tied to the core
descriptor rather than to the spelling of the name. With no operand run is a
usage error, and an uninstalled name is status 127. Reaching the run handler
itself means no script library was supplied, which it reports as Unavailable.

A call frame is the same machinery for a function body and for an installed
script; only the frame kind differs. It is the body's list plus ownership of the
caller's positional parameters. ShellState::push_positionals copies the
caller's slots into the call arena and installs the call's arguments above them
in the text pool; popping the frame restores the slots, the text cursor, and
the caller's shift count, so a callee's `$1` and its own `shift` never leak out.
The arena is bump-allocated and released in frame order.

Open calls are capped by EvaluatorStorage::call_limit. Exceeding it refuses the
call with status 2 and Overflow rather than exhausting the frame span, and the
refused call unwinds like any other failure.

### Command substitution

`$(list)` runs without a process. A simple command that contains one is entered
through a Substitute frame, which resolves each substitution before the command
expands its words. Each one parses its nested list into the capture parse arena,
forks a child ShellState into the capture variable storage, adopts the parent's
function handles into the capture function library, installs the capture sink,
and pushes a Capture frame. Everything the child needs is preflighted before its
first command runs. The Capture frame is an ordinary list frame, so the
application keeps control between the nested command and the command that
consumes its output, and a step still enters at most one handler.

The child receives copied variables, positional parameters, options, and status,
and adopted function handles. Its mutations never return to the parent: nothing
is committed back when the frame pops. Native side effects outside ShellState
are real, exactly as they are inside POSIX command substitution — a device write
inside `$()` still writes.

A substitution replaces only the ordinary-output sink. Diagnostics keep going to
the application, staged as usual. Capture capacity is enforced on each write;
the evaluator strips trailing LF bytes and publishes the remainder. Capture
overflow fails the containing expansion with StorageClass::CaptureBytes and
never publishes truncated text.

The capture text and value arenas are bump-allocated and released with the
Substitute frame that filled them, so several substitutions in one command
coexist. Words are visited in the order the evaluator expands them: the command
words first, then the assignment prefixes.

Two forms are refused rather than approximated at this level, each with
Status::Unsupported: a substitution inside another substitution, and a
substitution inside a parameter operand such as `${x:-$(y)}`. A `for` word list
or `case` selector containing one is refused the same way, because only a simple
command is entered through a Substitute frame. Leaving any capture span empty
also makes a substitution unsupported, which is how an application that does not
want nested execution declares that.

### Flow ownership

A flow value is consumed only by the frame that owns it. Break and Continue
locate the innermost While or For frame, popping the frames above it; Break
pops the loop and Continue re-enters it at its next test or item. A call frame
is a barrier to both, so a body that breaks with no loop of its own does not
reach the caller's loop. Return locates the innermost call frame, which is what
restores the caller's arguments. Exit abandons every open frame and returns
Complete. Yield returns Yielded with the frame stack intact, so the next step
resumes after the yielding command.

A flow value with no owning frame leaves the stack untouched and reports status
2: Break or Continue outside a loop, and Return outside a function or installed
script. Replace stays unowned at this level, because it requires the level-3
process service.

tested_context reports whether the command that last ran occupied a tested
context: a condition list, an inverted command, or a non-final operand of
`&&` or `||`. The flag propagates into compound commands, so the body of a
brace group used as an `if` condition is tested too.

### set -e and set -u

set -e reads that flag rather than a bare nonzero status. A command that fails
outside a tested context drops every open frame and ends the evaluator with
that status, which step reports as Complete exactly as `exit` would. A failing
condition, an inverted command, a non-final `&&` or `||` operand, and anything
inside them are exempt; a loop body is not.

set -u makes an unset parameter an error inside expansion, and that error ends
the evaluator with status 1 and Status::NotFound whether or not -e is set. A
`:-`, `:+`, or `:=` operand is exempt because the parameter is never unset by
the time it is used. A `for` word list and a `case` selector report it the same
way a simple command does, rather than as an evaluator fault. Without -u an
unset parameter still expands to nothing.

### Output staging and I/O retry

A handler never writes to a partial physical transport. The evaluator presents
an all-or-fail staging sink through the CommandContext it builds for the
dispatch, over the caller's staged_output and staged_error spans. An empty span
makes that stream write straight through, which suits a memory sink that never
blocks.

After a handler finishes, the evaluator retains its completed CommandResult and
drains the staged bytes over later step calls. Each step performs at most one
bounded drain advance, the undrained remainder of one stream. WouldBlock
consumes no byte and is retried; Failed ends the pending command with the
transport's own failure. The retained result is delivered only once its bytes
have reached the application, so step returns Running while output is pending
and no further command is selected. A partial transport write therefore never
re-enters a handler and never repeats a physical side effect. pending_output
reports that state.

Staging capacity smaller than a handler's output is an application sizing
error, not a retry case: the staging sink refuses with Overflow and
StorageClass::StagedOutput, the handler stops there, and only the bytes it had
already handed over are drained.

A handler is entered exactly once per command whatever the transport does. A
fake bounded transaction proves it: with a transport that refuses three writes
before accepting, the handler records one entry and one physical effect, and
the retried drain repeats neither. The same fake proves the scratch contract,
returning Overflow with StorageClass::TransactionScratch, no output, and no
effect when its span is too small.

Every level-1 language construct is implemented, the standard pack installs
atomically, and apps/shell-smoke runs the fixed boundary script against the
named acceptance storage profile. Its big-endian M68k and little-endian MPS2
QEMU executions both pass. The level-1 RP2040 flash budget is a separate
release-size gate, not a host test.

## Session in progress

Session accumulates source a byte at a time into caller storage and runs each
complete command through one Evaluator. SessionStorage carries the source span,
the parser's ScriptStorage, and the EvaluatorStorage. An EmbeddedScript views
the source span, so it must outlive execution and must not alias parser or
evaluator storage.

feed distinguishes a complete command from an open one with the parser's
Incomplete result, so an `if`, loop, `case`, function, or brace group spanning
several physical lines accumulates until it closes. The states are Ready,
Continuing, Executing, and Discarding.

- a physical line ending measures the buffer. Complete parses it and starts the
  evaluator, returning the byte offset just past that terminator so the caller
  resumes the rest of its buffer after the script finishes; Incomplete keeps
  accumulating; Malformed is reported once with its location and the buffer is
  discarded unexecuted;
- an empty physical line is a no-op rather than a parse error;
- backspace and delete remove one buffered byte;
- printable bytes and horizontal tab are buffered. Tab is accepted beyond the
  recognized set of the language specification because dropping it would join
  adjacent tokens. Every other control byte is ignored;
- an overlong command reports Overflow with StorageClass::SourceBytes exactly
  once, then discards through the next physical line ending. It is never
  executed as a truncated script;
- feed is refused with Unavailable while a script is executing.

step forwards to the evaluator and reclaims the source buffer only when the
script reaches Complete or Failed, which is the point at which nothing views
those bytes any more. Session owns no history, completion, cursor movement,
ANSI handling, or transport loop: the application polls its transport and
alternates feed and step.

## Core builtins in progress

core_builtins materializes the eighteen level-1 core descriptors into
caller-owned slots: `:`, true, false, echo, printf, test, `[`, set, shift,
break, continue, return, exit, yield, capability, help, command, and run. It is
not an installation; it exists for introspection and for focused tests that
dispatch descriptors directly. A short slot span publishes nothing and returns
Overflow with StorageClass::CustomCommands.

install_level1 is how an application obtains the pack. It installs all eighteen
descriptors into a registry, all or none, through the internal entry point that
admits the special-builtin class, and sets the Introspection's registry for
help and command. It materializes the pack on the stack because the registry
copies every descriptor it accepts and each one views only static text plus the
caller's binding.

An application installs it before any custom command, so a custom name cannot
take one the pack owns: a later install of `echo` is Duplicate. Installing the
pack twice is refused without changing the registry, reported as Overflow when
the registry has no room for a second pack and as Duplicate when it does,
because the capacity check precedes duplicate detection.

`:`, break, continue, exit, return, set, and shift carry
CommandClass::SpecialBuiltin, which is what prefix-assignment persistence and
name resolution read. Registry::install refuses that class, so focused tests
dispatch the descriptors directly.

help and command need the tables they describe, so core_builtins binds them to
an Introspection the caller owns, holding the registry and an optional script
library. It must outlive the descriptors. A null script library simply leaves
installed scripts out of the listing. Every other core descriptor is
stateless.

Behavior notes at this level:

- echo writes its operands separated by one space and one trailing LF;
- printf supports literal text, `%s`, `%%`, and the `\\`, `\n`, `\t`, `\r`,
  `\a`, `\b`, `\f`, and `\v` escapes, reuses the format while conversions
  keep consuming operands, expands a missing operand to nothing, and refuses
  any other conversion with Status::Unsupported;
- test and `[` accept `!`, -n, -z, =, !=, -eq, -ne, -lt, -le, -gt, and -ge,
  comparing numbers through the checked 64-bit parser. `[` requires its
  closing `]`. An unknown operator or unparsable number is status 2;
- set validates every option before changing a flag, so a rejected operand
  leaves errexit and nounset exactly as they were;
- shift defaults to one, accepts a std::uint32_t decimal count, succeeds
  without change on zero, and returns 1 leaving every argument in place when
  the count exceeds `$#`;
- break and continue take no loop-count operand at this level;
- return and exit default to the current status and accept 0 through 255;
- capability lists the served capability names or queries one, with status 1
  for an unserved name and status 2 for an unknown one;
- help lists the native table and then the installed scripts, or describes one
  named command or script;
- command resolves a name with -v or dispatches it, reporting 127 when the
  name is not installed. It looks only at native commands, so a function or
  script never shadows what it selects;
- run is resolved by the evaluator, as described under the evaluator.

A sink that refuses bytes stops the handler at that point. WouldBlock is
reported as Status::Unavailable and a broken sink as its own failure status,
so no handler re-writes bytes it already delivered. Staged output with retry
arrives with Session.

# Platform lane qualification

The level-1 mm.shell foundation and apps/shell-smoke fixture qualification
evidence across the registered platform directories under platforms/:

- linux: host execution and native compilation (all 18 unit tests pass;
  apps/shell-smoke compiled, linked, installed to out/bin, and executed).
- arm-linux-gnueabihf: target compilation and link (apps/shell-smoke compiled
  and linked to 32-bit ARM ELF executable).
- m68k-linux-gnu: target compilation and link (apps/shell-smoke compiled and
  linked to 32-bit big-endian ELF executable via m68k-linux-gnu-g++-15).
- mps2-an385: target compilation and link (apps/shell-smoke compiled and linked
  for Cortex-M3 with semihosted specs and link.ld).
- pico: target compilation and link via external CMake bridge (apps/shell-smoke
  compiled, linked to RP2040 ELF, and valid UF2 image generated).
- pico2-arm: target compilation and link via external CMake bridge
  (apps/shell-smoke compiled, linked to RP2350 Arm Cortex-M33 ELF, and valid UF2
  image generated).
- aarch64-linux-gnu: not run: SDK sysroot /usr/aarch64-linux-gnu is not
  installed in the host environment.
- pico2-riscv: not run: riscv32-pico-elf-g++ compiler is not installed in the
  host environment.
- m68k-linux-external: recorded as an external CMake fixture rather than a
  project-module lane.
